# dash/safe.bd - trace and IDS verdicts.
#
# IT REPORTS. IT NEVER ACTS. This is the whole design rule.
#
# The old shell/ suite's check_trace() called disconnect()/exit()/raise()
# inline. That is fine in a one-shot script and fatal in a long-running
# panel: exit() would kill the dashboard from inside a helper, and raise()
# would unwind the event loop past everything holding the UI together. So
# every function here returns a verdict string and the loop decides what to
# do about it.
#
# All reads are 0 ticks (get_trace_time, ids_status). The only cost is the
# lines themselves, which is why this is one function and not five.
#
# VERDICTS, worst first:
#   abort     IDS is actively watching - get off the box now
#   hopeless  trace ceiling is too low to do anything useful with
#   hot       trace is running out; disconnect and let it decay
#   flagged   noticed but not yet urgent
#   ok        proceed
#
# "hot" was called "cool" until it kept being read as reassurance. It is the
# opposite: the trace is nearly up, leave. The waiting-to-recover phase in
# dash.bd is called "cooling", and having those two sound related while
# meaning opposite things was a trap worth removing.

# Thresholds are PASSED IN, not held here. They are seconds remaining on the
# live trace, and the right values depend on the box - a 20s ceiling needs
# very different numbers from a 120s one, which is why they became a setting
# rather than an edit to this file.
#
# The caller is responsible for keeping hot > hopeless. check() tests
# hopeless first, so equal values make "hot" unreachable dead code.
#
# These remain only as the fallback the dashboard starts from.
var HOPELESS = 5
var HOT = 10

# Judges the BOX, from its trace ceiling alone - "is this worth being on at
# all", answerable the moment a connection opens and before any work starts.
#
# A CEILING OF 0 MEANS THE TRACE COMPLETES INSTANTLY: connecting gets you
# caught. It is the worst case there is, not an absence of danger. Anything
# reading a low ceiling as "safe" has the sign backwards - which is easy to
# do, because 0 also looks like "nothing running" everywhere else.
func ceiling_verdict(ceiling, hopeless):
    if ceiling <= 0:
        return "instant"
    if ceiling <= hopeless:
        return "hopeless"
    return "ok"

# Judges the LIVE SESSION. `ceiling` is connect()'s trace_time, captured when
# the session opened; `t` is what is left right now.
func check(conn, ceiling, hopeless, hot):
    var t = get_trace_time()
    var i = ids_status(conn)
    var v = "ok"
    if i == "active":
        v = "abort"
    elif ceiling <= 0:
        v = "instant"
    elif t <= hopeless:
        v = "hopeless"
    elif t <= hot:
        v = "hot"
    elif i == "flagged":
        v = "flagged"
    return {"trace": t, "ids": i, "verdict": v}
